Reject what an app outside the VPN sends through the tun, behind
keqdroid-owner-guard.

An app excluded from the VPN (addDisallowedApplication) can still bind a
socket to tun0: SO_BINDTODEVICE needs no rights since Linux 5.7. Its packets
then come out of our tun, go through the server, and the app learns the
server address. The system knows such a socket, but getConnectionOwnerUid
answers INVALID_UID to the VPN app for any UID the VPN does not cover, so
mihomo-android-uid-owner.patch got an empty owner and the connection went on
like any other.

The app now answers "keqdroid:outside-vpn" for INVALID_UID on Android 10+
(ConnectionOwnerServer.kt). With keqdroid-owner-guard set, that answer is the
process name, and keqdroid writes
AND,((IN-TYPE,TUN),(PROCESS-NAME,keqdroid:outside-vpn)),REJECT as the first
rule. Without the key it stays an unknown owner, as before.

With keqdroid-owner-guard: all the DNS hijack asks as well. It answers before
any rule runs, and it sends TXT and CH queries upstream through the server:
"whoami.cloudflare TXT CH" sent into the tun came back with the server
address. "connections" leaves DNS alone: with "only selected apps" the
system's own DNS and Private DNS (DoT from AID_DNS) come with no owner the
VPN may see, and rejecting them would take DNS away from the selected apps.
keqdroid lets port 853 through in the rule then too.

No answer from the app (socket closed, app gone) lets the connection
through: the guard rejects only what the system named as not the VPN's.

Applies after mihomo-android-uid-owner.patch and mihomo-udp-per-exit.patch,
which touch the same files; the name keeps it sorted after both.

